The lights cut with a *thunk* Dario felt in his teeth, and the car dropped into a dark that
smelled like hot brake dust and someone's spilled coffee gone sour on the floor. The train
groaned once, a long metal exhale, and stopped.

"Great," said the woman across from him. Mid-thirties, black hair scraped into a knot with a
pen shoved through it, a courier bag clamped across her chest like she expected someone to grab
it. Olive coat two sizes big, cuffs frayed, one button hanging by a thread she kept worrying
between her fingers. Runner's build under the coat, shoulders squared, a thin white scar
splitting her left eyebrow. *Don't look at the bag. Don't look at it and he won't either.*

"You get a lot of these?" Dario asked. Emergency lights bled on, dim and red, painting them
both the color of a darkroom.

"Enough." She didn't look up.

"You were counting stops." He hadn't meant to say it — it just came out, the way things do when
the dark makes strangers honest. "Since Bergen. Lips moving. Like you had somewhere you needed
to be at an exact time."

Her fingers stopped on the button. "People count when they're nervous."

"Nervous people don't check the same watch every ninety seconds." He nodded at her wrist — a
man's watch, too big, face turned to the inside like she was hiding it. "That's not yours."

For a second the only sound was cooling metal ticking overhead and, far down the tunnel, water
dripping into water, *plink... plink.* She finally lifted her eyes to him, steadier than her
hands.

"You a cop?"

"Electrician." He held up his palms, calloused, a burn scar glazed across the right one. "I fix
things that carry current. I know what a person looks like when they're waiting for something to
go off."

The button came loose in her fingers. She closed her fist around it. "Then you know," she said
quietly, "that the worst thing you can do to a live line is grab it to see if it's hot."

The train shuddered. Somewhere ahead a breaker slammed home and the overheads flickered,
threatening to come back. She was already standing, bag hugged tighter, watching the doors like
they were a countdown, and Dario stayed exactly where he was, hands open on his knees, watching
her decide whether he was a wire she had to cut.

